Dīgha: mindfulness and lay relationships (@buddhist-dn-mindfulness-and-relationships: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @buddhism, #source @buddhist-source-long-discourses, #philosophy @buddhist-communal-harmony, #buddhism @buddhist-communal-harmony) { r_713MZQTGM1CRFPARAJW19EAZGT
**Read:** DN 22 and DN 31 in [The Long Discourses of the Buddha](../sources/Long-discourses.lingua#r_4A7XBPNASHTXPADP129ZBZAGRC), completing the selected corpus. Read additional discourses when a contextual or doctrinal question needs them; record which ones you use.

Compare DN 22’s presentation of mindfulness with the account already encountered in the earlier collections. Examine how DN 31 presents responsibilities within relationships, including what its categories assume about the household and social world.

**Investigate:** select a duty within one relationship. Distinguish the purpose it serves, the social arrangement it presupposes, and your judgment about applying it now. A contemporary endorsement or objection needs reasons beyond quoting an ancient prescription.

**Check:** explain a connection between contemplation, conduct, and communal life without reducing any of them to the other. Preserve the distinction between this selected Dīgha reading and completing all its discourses. Carry the question of social application into Bodhi’s anthology.

**Continue:** [Communal harmony: disagreement, cooperation, and application](Communal-harmony.lingua#r_1BKX3CXYEZ43X3E40A5J7QERN6).

**Return:** [Buddhism: from the path to sustained inquiry](../Buddhism.lingua#r_7P61Z6HHZNZZ8ND3DHXSFSNRRE).
